// Registry 825. This file REALLY begins with U+FEFF -- byte 0 is 0xEF -- which is
// what a Windows editor writes by default. D104 (spec/decisions/03-syntax.md:6171)
// is normative: "UTF-8 only. A BOM at the start of the file is stripped before
// doc-recognition." Nothing stripped it, so this file used to die with
// `unexpected byte`, naming a character the source never contained (the first BOM
// byte read as Latin-1) -- and the snippet drew line 1 as perfectly valid, because
// the caret pointed at a byte a reader cannot see.
// EXPECT_STDOUT bom stripped
//
// The marker deliberately sits on the SECOND line: the BOM has to be at byte 0, and
// a marker line beginning with an invisible character would test the runner's
// tolerance rather than the compiler's.
module standalone.m825_bom_at_start

fn main() Io -> () {
    println("bom stripped")
}
